{"primaryContentSections":[{"declarations":[{"platforms":["macOS"],"languages":["swift"],"tokens":[{"text":"enum","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"Kind","kind":"identifier"}]}],"kind":"declarations"},{"content":[{"type":"heading","level":2,"text":"Overview","anchor":"overview"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"In start-stop cases, ties can add more elements using a continue type. This is typically used to specify the","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"formatting of cross-system ties.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"When multiple elements with the same tag are used within the same note, their order within the MusicXML","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"document should match the musical score order. For example, a note with a tie at the end of a first ending","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"should have the tied element with a type of start precede the tied element with a type of stop.","type":"text"}]}],"kind":"content"}],"identifier":{"url":"doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLTied\/Kind-swift.enum","interfaceLanguage":"swift"},"sections":[],"kind":"symbol","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"The tied-type type is used as an attribute of the tied element to specify where the visual representation"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"of a tie begins and ends. A tied element which joins two notes of the same pitch can be specified with"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"tied-type start on the first note and tied-type stop on the second note. To indicate a note should be"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"undamped, use a single tied element with tied-type let-ring. For other ties that are visually attached to a"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"single note, such as a tie leading into or out of a repeated section or coda, use two tied elements on the"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"